@page "/expivot/layout"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Pivot


<DocsPage Product="ExPivot" Title="Layout"
          Lead="Which fields stand where, how the rows are set out, which Items are shown, collapsed or ordered: all of it is the Pivot Layout, which the user changes in the pane and your code can set directly.">

    <DocsSection Title="The PivotTable Fields pane">
        <p>
            The pane beside the report is Excel's. Its top half lists the source's fields, its bottom half
            the four Areas: <strong>Filters</strong>, <strong>Columns</strong>, <strong>Rows</strong> and
            <strong>Values</strong>. The report below starts with nothing placed.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Tick a field</strong> and it goes where Excel puts it: a number into Values, summed;
                text, a date or a Boolean into Rows. Untick it to take it out of every Area.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Drag a field</strong> from the list into an Area, or an entry from one Area to another,
                or within an Area to change its order. A field stands in only one of Filters, Rows and Columns:
                dropped into another of them, it moves there with its settings. Dropped into Values, it becomes
                one more Value Field, so Values may hold the same field twice. With two or more Value Fields,
                a <strong>Σ Values</strong> entry appears, which moves between Columns and Rows.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Each placed entry has a menu</strong> (the ▾ beside it): move it up, down or to another
                Area, remove it, and open <em>Field Settings…</em> (subtotals, Items' order) or
                <em>Value Field Settings…</em> (the Aggregation, Show Values As, the number format and the
                caption). A field in Filters shows above the report, where its drop-down picks the Items.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Every drag has a keyboard route.</strong> Space on a field's checkbox places it, and Enter
                on an entry's ▾ opens the menu that moves it. A menu or a panel takes the keyboard as it opens,
                with the keys typed on its button meanwhile: Enter typed straight after the Enter that opens a
                field's menu, or Layout ▾, presses its first command rather than closing it again, and letters
                typed straight after Enter on a report filter above the report are typed into its search while the
                report is shown. With no report to carry them — only report filters placed, or a details tab over the
                report — they are dropped rather than typed elsewhere, a known limitation of <code>0.1.0</code>; Escape
                typed then still closes the panel.
                Escape closes it, a panel's changes are discarded unless OK applied them, and the keyboard goes
                back to what opened it, with the keys typed as it closes: Enter typed straight after Escape opens the
                menu again rather than running one of its commands. Both hold over Blazor Server too.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Defer Layout Update</strong>, at the pane's foot, holds the pane's changes until you
                press Update, as Excel does for a slow source. The report is not asked meanwhile.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>The Pivot Toolbar</strong> above the report holds the Filters band, the
                <em>Layout ▾</em> menu and the pane's toggle. Bind <code>ShowFieldList</code> to remember
                whether the user hid the pane.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(FieldsPane)" Mud="typeof(FieldsPaneMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Pivot/Sales.cs"])">
            Tick Country and Revenue, then drag Year from the list into Columns.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Report forms, subtotals and grand totals">
        <p>
            The <em>Layout ▾</em> menu on the Pivot Toolbar offers Excel's choices: Compact, Outline or Tabular
            form, item labels repeated or not, subtotals at the top or the bottom of their group or none, and
            the grand totals on or off for rows and columns. Each is a property of <code>PivotLayout</code>,
            so your code can set them as well.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Compact</strong>, Excel's default: one indented label column, a group row per outer Item.</li>
            <li><strong>Outline</strong>: a label column per row field, still with a group row per outer Item.</li>
            <li><strong>Tabular</strong>: a label column per row field and no group rows; subtotals always at the bottom.</li>
            <li>
                <code>GrandTotalRow</code> is the <em>Grand Total</em> row at the bottom (Excel's grand totals for
                columns); <code>GrandTotalColumn</code> is the column at the right (grand totals for rows).
                A field's own subtotals are switched in its Field Settings (<code>PivotFieldPlacement.Subtotals</code>).
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Select cells of the report and the line under it shows Excel's Average, Count and Sum of what you
            selected. As in Excel, a subtotal or a grand total you select is summed with the rest — select a whole
            column and the total counts its parts twice. An error value among them leaves Count and Numerical
            Count, as in Excel, and the figures wear the format of the Focus cell's Value Field.
            <code>ShowSelectionSummary="false"</code> turns the line off.
        </p>
        <p>
            The report's header shows which columns the selection holds: each such header is shaded, its letters
            coloured, and a line two device pixels thick drawn along its bottom edge. This includes the pinned
            label column when the selection reaches it. A whole column takes a distinct shade, including when
            several ranges together cover it. It is ExGrid's
            <a href="exgrid/selection#rectangles--a-focus-and-an-extent">Heading Mark</a>:
            <code>--ex-heading-mark</code> colours the line and letters in the Focus outline's colour unless set;
            <code>--ex-heading-mark-background</code> and <code>--ex-heading-mark-whole-background</code> set the
            two shades. Your Theme's tokens win, ExPivot.MudBlazor uses its palette, and the header follows the
            frame's colour scheme. Where the header covers a side of the Focus or a single range, the mark supplies
            the two device pixels without a second outline beside it. Against the pinned block, the outline stays
            inside the cell.
            The ordinary Focus and single-range outline defaults to two device pixels on every side,
            including at display scales above 100%.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ReportForms)" Mud="typeof(ReportFormsMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Pivot/Sales.cs"])">
            Select a cell, then Shift+click another to mark the columns between them. Click a report header to
            select its whole column and compare the shade. Try Ctrl+A and switch between Built-in and MudBlazor.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Expand and collapse, Hidden Items, sorting">
        <p>
            Each field placed in Filters, Rows or Columns is a <code>PivotFieldPlacement</code> carrying its
            own settings, which travel with it when it moves between those Areas.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Expand and collapse.</strong> The <code>−</code> and <code>+</code> beside an outer Item
                collapse it to one row carrying its totals; <em>Expand / Collapse Entire Field</em> in the
                Context Menu does a whole field. A collapsed Item still counts in every total.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Hidden Items.</strong> The Items the user unticks in <em>Filter…</em> leave their records
                out of the report, every total included, as in Excel. That is a filter, not a hidden row.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Sorting.</strong> A field's Items are ordered by label, A to Z, unless set otherwise:
                by label descending, or by a Value Field's total (<code>PivotSort.ByValue</code>). The Context
                Menu on a value offers <em>Sort Largest to Smallest</em> and <em>Smallest to Largest</em>.
                <code>(blank)</code> is last either way. A text field may declare Excel's custom list
                (<code>itemOrder</code>, as Channel does in <code>Sales.cs</code>) or an Order Key.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            ExPivot never sorts or filters the report grid itself: a header click selects the column, and the
            grid's column menu is left out. Item order and Hidden Items belong to the layout.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ItemsAndOrder)" Mud="typeof(ItemsAndOrderMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Pivot/Sales.cs"])">
            Regions collapsed except Europe, countries ordered by revenue, and Direct sales hidden.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Find">
        <p>
            <kbd>Ctrl+F</kbd> in the report opens the report grid's find panel, as in
            <a href="exgrid/sorting-filtering#with-gridsource-from">ExGrid</a>, and Enter (Shift+Enter backward) moves
            the Focus to the next match. ExPivot answers it over the report <strong>as shown</strong>, as Excel's Find
            over a PivotTable reads the cells on the sheet.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Every cell the report lays out</strong> is searched: the row labels, the values, the subtotals
                and the grand totals, the rows beyond the ones on screen included. A cell matches on its
                <strong>displayed text</strong>, its number format applied (<code>1,235</code>, not the
                <code>1234.5</code> behind it), by ExGrid's rule: case ignored unless <em>Match case</em>, contained
                unless <em>Whole cell</em>. With more than one cell selected, only the selection is searched.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>A collapsed group is not searched inside, and nothing is expanded to show a match.</strong>
                Its rows are not laid out, so they are not found, and a search never changes the report's shape.
                Expand the group to search it.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>The column labels are not searched yet.</strong> The column Items and the value captions
                stand in the report grid's headers, and Find lands only on cells, so a text that appears only there
                is not found. Whether, and how, Find reaches them is still to be decided.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>The matches on screen are marked</strong> as you type, the current one apart, and the rows
                scrolled into view are marked as they are painted: ExPivot judges them by the rule it finds by, through
                ExGrid's Match Mark, in the Visual Tokens <code>--ex-match-mark-fill</code> and
                <code>--ex-match-mark-current-fill</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>There is no Replace.</strong> A report is computed, not written, so nothing in it can be replaced:
                <kbd>Ctrl+H</kbd> opens nothing, and the Pivot Toolbar says, in Excel's words, <em>Cannot change this
                part of a PivotTable report.</em> (in Japanese under <code>PivotWords.Japanese</code>).
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Nothing is wired for it. Over a server's report, the rows beyond the Window are read through the
            <code>copy</code> transport of <code>PivotReportSource.Fetch</code>, by the Report Version on screen, a block
            of rows at a time, until the match. A step that cannot read the report — the source refuses, answers
            another version than asked, or no longer holds the report on screen — moves nothing and says why on the
            Pivot Toolbar, never "No match". A live update while a step reads is searched again over the report now
            shown; a report that keeps changing is said, and you find again.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(FindInReport)" Mud="typeof(FindInReportMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Pivot/Sales.cs"])">
            Click the report, press Ctrl+F and type "laptop": only Europe's rows are found and marked, because the other
            regions are collapsed. Then type a revenue figure as it is shown, with its thousands separator.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="An Order Key on a server">
        <p>
            An Order Key orders a field's Items by a key it gives each one, rather than by label: over local data,
            the field declares the function (<code>orderKey:</code>). A report a server computes cannot be handed a
            function from the browser, so the server registers its Order Keys by name,
            <code>PivotReportSource.From(data, orderKeys: new Dictionary&lt;string, Func&lt;object, IComparable?&gt;&gt; { ["market-size"] = … })</code>,
            and ExPivot's <code>OrderKeyPolicies</code> names the one each field is ordered by
            (<code>["Region"] = "market-size"</code>). Only the name crosses the wire. A name the server has not
            registered, and a function that throws, are refused by name, field and Item included; the Items are
            never ordered by label instead. Changing <code>OrderKeyPolicies</code> on a report already shown asks
            for the report again, as a new layout does, so the Items follow the policy the parameter names.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ServerOrderKey)" Mud="typeof(ServerOrderKeyMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Pivot/SalesServer.cs", "Examples/Pivot/Sales.cs"])">
            The regions in order of their market's size, largest first, by a function the simulated server registered.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

